{"skill": "Two Passages", "difficulty": "Hard", "question": "**Passage 1:** Dr. Li, a conservation ecologist, contends that biodiversity is the foremost determinant of ecosystem stability, arguing that a high number of species acts as a protective shield against environmental disruptions. Drawing on studies of historically lush forests that harbored a diverse array of organisms, Dr. Li maintains that ecosystem collapse almost invariably follows even a moderate loss of species richness. She warns that biodiversity is not merely beneficial but essential: to ensure resilience against catastrophes such as invasive diseases and extreme climate events, ecosystems must retain the greatest possible breadth of species. Any strategy that prioritizes just a handful of valuable species, according to Dr. Li, ignores the crucial roles played by less prominent organisms, leaving the entire ecosystem vulnerable.

**Passage 2:** Professor Mathur, an ecosystem scientist, provides a qualified view of biodiversity’s role in maintaining stability. Although she acknowledges that decreasing species diversity can compromise an ecosystem’s resiliency, Mathur’s recent grassland studies reveal that some communities remain robust so long as their essential species are preserved. She identifies these species as keystone organisms, responsible for critical tasks such as controlling nutrient cycles or regulating prey populations. In one nutrient-enrichment experiment, grassland biodiversity declined, yet the ecosystem’s core functions continued without major disruption because keystone species prevailed. According to Mathur, while biodiversity is advantageous, an ecosystem’s fate may hinge on safeguarding those select organisms whose disappearance would trigger widespread collapse.

Based on the two passages, how would Mathur (Passage 2) most likely respond to Li’s (Passage 1) conclusion that the only reliable way to preserve ecosystem stability is to protect every species in an ecosystem?", "choices": ["A. She would adopt Li’s assertion that every single species, regardless of its ecological role, is equally critical to an ecosystem’s stability.", "B. She would acknowledge biodiversity’s importance overall but emphasize that preserving certain key species may ensure stability even if some less crucial species are lost.", "C. She would dispute the idea that biodiversity contributes to ecological resilience, contending instead that random environmental factors primarily determine stability.", "D. She would insist that protecting significant temperature ranges and climatic conditions should supersede any concern about species variety."], "answer": "B", "explanation": "Passage 1 claims that to maintain stability, an ecosystem must safeguard as many species as possible, implying that every member of the community is indispensable. Passage 2 agrees that species diversity generally aids ecosystem resilience but adds the nuance that certain keystone organisms can uphold stability even when overall biodiversity decreases. Because Professor Mathur highlights how sustaining these key species can avert collapse, her position suggests that wide-ranging biodiversity, though beneficial, may not be the sole path to resilience. This directly responds to Dr. Li’s more absolute claim. While Dr. Li insists on all-encompassing protection, Mathur believes that, in some cases, targeted conservation of the most crucial species can suffice. By recognizing the significance of certain organisms while not outright rejecting the value of diversity, Mathur offers a more measured stance than Li’s position.", "topic": "The relationship between biodiversity and ecosystem stability"}

