{"skill": "Geometry", "difficulty": "Hard", "question": "Consider a circle with center $O$. Distinct points $A$, $B$, $C$, and $D$ appear on the circle in that order. The measure of arc $AB$ is $80^\circ$, the measure of arc $BC$ is $90^\circ$, the measure of arc $CD$ is $80^\circ$, and the measure of arc $DA$ is $110^\circ$, so that the four arcs sum to $360^\circ$.

Chords $AC$ and $BD$ intersect at point $H$ inside the circle. If the measure of $\angle AHB$ is $80^\circ$, what is the measure of $\angle ADB$?", "choices": ["A. 85", "B. 75", "C. 90", "D. 100"], "answer": "A", "explanation": "First, recall the fact that when two chords of a circle intersect at a point inside the circle, the measure of the angle formed is half the sum of the measures of the arcs intercepted by that angle and its vertical angle. In this problem, $\angle AHB$ intercepts arcs $AB$ and $CD$. Because $AB$ has measure $80^\circ$ and $CD$ has measure $80^\circ$, their sum is $160^\circ$. Thus, $\angle AHB$ is half of $160^\circ$, which confirms $80^\circ$.

Next, to find $\angle ADB$, notice that it is an inscribed angle intercepting arc $ACB$, which is the sum of arcs $AB$ and $BC$. Since arc $AB$ measures $80^\circ$ and arc $BC$ measures $90^\circ$, arc $ACB$ measures $170^\circ$. As an inscribed angle, $\angle ADB$ is half of $170^\circ$, which is $85^\circ$. Thus, the measure of $\angle ADB$ is $85^\circ$.", "topic": "Circle geometry with chords"}

